WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:04.300
Just because many screenwriters, directors
and stars are way to the left

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:08.180
doesn't mean that films with libertarian themes
and messages don't get made.

00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:14.040
Here are 5 great movies made over the past
25 years that any libertarian will enjoy.

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:20.560
This Pixar film directed by Brad Bird is so
full of speeches

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:24.220
extolling individualism, personal greatness
and self fulfillment,

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:26.960
it sometimes sounds like it was scripted by
Ayn Rand.

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:34.300
You sly dog, you got me monologuing, I can't
believe it.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:38.600
In fact Rand is clearly part of the inspiration
for the character of Edna Mode.

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:39.480
No capes!

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:42.560
The libertarianism of the film exists on at
least two levels:

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:45.220
one obvious and one a little more hidden.

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:50.000
But even the super villain in The Incredibles
is a creature of self invention and self improvement.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.100
While the Incredibles are born with their
powers,

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:55.160
Syndrome is a normie who worships Mr Incredible

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:57.360
and is desperate to be his side kick.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:01.480
I know all your moves, favorite crime fighting
style, favorite catch phrases, everything!

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:03.080
I am your number one fan!

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:08.039
When Mr Incredible dismisses young Buddy,

00:01:08.039 --> 00:01:11.140
he uses that disillusionment to fire his ambitions

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:14.480
to create weapons and powers via innovative
technology.

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:19.660
Like an animated version of Richard Nixon,
syndrome's ambitions ultimately get the best of him.

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:24.540
I mean, after all, I am your biggest fan.

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:27.240
But he just may be the real hero of the movie,

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:30.880
at least for those of us without naturally
occurring super powers.

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:35.980
The Incredibles are returning after a 14 year
hiatus with a sequel hitting theaters in June.

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:38.660
So we'll see them again soon.

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:43.299
French Canadian director Denys Arcand's brilliant
sequel to The Decline of The American Empire

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:47.380
is the single best depiction of the depredations
of socialized medicine.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.540
The healthcare system in the movie is so sclerotic,

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:58.760
that the movie's protagonist, a retired academic
named Remy,

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:02.020
cannot even score the drugs he needs to commit
suicide,

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:05.820
until his estranged banker son buys them on
the black market.

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:15.280
Even more disturbing is the moment when the
terminally ill Remy

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:17.620
and his former university colleagues

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:19.940
admit that their intellectual faddishness

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:24.760
led them to embrace every awful left wing
ism of the past 30 years

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:27.600
despite their often massive human toll.

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:36.320
None of those drugs have been approved by
the FDA.

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:40.080
Screw the FDA, I'm gonna be DOA.

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:43.500
This based on a true story movie about a rodeo
rider and a drag queen

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:46.060
riding around the US Food and Drug Administration

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:48.940
brought home Oscars not just for it's two
leads,

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:53.960
but the film's makeup artist who reportedly
worked on a budget of just 250 dollars.

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:56.980
Set in the 1980s Matthew McConaughey plays
Ron Woodroof

00:02:56.980 --> 00:03:02.020
a boozy roughneck who is given 30 days to
live after being diagnosed with AIDS.

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:05.980
Faced with such a death sentence, he schools
himself on a wide variety of treatments

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:08.880
first in Mexico and then all over the world.

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:11.740
With the help of a cross dressing party girl
named Rayon,

00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:17.099
Woodroof skirts FDA prohibitions against importing,
using and selling unapproved drugs

00:03:17.099 --> 00:03:21.030
by creating a buyer's club in which members
pay a monthly membership

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:22.560
and assume all risks.

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:25.829
I'm afraid that you are nothing more than
a common drug dealer,

00:03:25.829 --> 00:03:27.580
so if you'll excuse us, we would like to-

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:28.500
Oh I'm the drug dealer?

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:33.020
No you're the fuckin' drug dealer, I mean
goddamn people are dying!

00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:35.320
The depiction of indifference to patient's
suffering

00:03:35.329 --> 00:03:37.810
and bureaucratic quashing of medical freedom

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:43.900
even for people who are certain to die is
unsparing, deeply moving and totally inspirational.

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:48.080
Especially now that even Donald Trump has
endorsed right to try legislation

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:52.380
that would allow terminally ill patients access
to non approved medicines.

00:03:57.540 --> 00:03:59.320
My name's Joy by the way.

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:02.480
Jennifer Lawrence became a mega star

00:04:02.489 --> 00:04:07.010
playing the anti government rebel Katniss
Everdeen in the Hunger Games movies.

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:10.780
And while there's no shortage of libertarian
sentiment coursing through that trilogy,

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:15.340
it's actually a quieter movie starring Lawrence
that fully embodies libertarian virtues

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:19.460
of hard work, commercial innovation and dogged
entrepreneurship.

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:24.640
In Joy, Lawrence plays real life Miracle Mop inventor
Joy Mangano

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:27.850
who helped make cleaning your floors a little
bit easier and better

00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:30.450
while becoming wealthy at the same time.

00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:36.030
The film is nothing less than a paean to capitalism's
genius at allowing self expression.

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:40.140
As the story begins, Joy is supporting a wide
variety of family layabouts

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:41.960
while barely making ends meet.

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:45.500
But she's firm with her daughter about what
really counts.

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:49.840
In a dramatic scene with Bradley Cooper who
plays an executive at a home shopping network,

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:51.840
Joy summarizes in a few sentences

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:56.500
what it took Joseph Schumpeter, Friedrich
Hayek and Milton Friedman whole books to say.

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:59.540
You said to me that David Selznick the son
of immigrants

00:04:59.540 --> 00:05:03.280
married Jennifer Jones an all American girl
from Oklahoma

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:10.520
because in America all races and all classes
can meet and make whatever opportunities they can

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:16.440
and that is what you feel when you reach into
people's homes with what you sell.

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:19.340
As former Reason editor Virginia Postrel wrote
of Joy:

00:05:19.340 --> 00:05:23.420
The film acknowledges the wealth creating
value of incremental improvements

00:05:23.420 --> 00:05:25.620
even in the most mundane items.

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:31.940
When it was first released in 1984,

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:36.580
Ghostbusters quickly became the most successful
comedy in film history.

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:38.760
In many ways the film was perfectly in sync

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:43.820
with the Reagan revolution's valorization
of business and demonization of government.

00:05:43.820 --> 00:05:46.500
You've never been out of college, you don't
know what it's like out there.

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:50.040
I've worked in the private sector, they expect
results.

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:53.260
Ghostbusters begins with a team of paranormal
investigators

00:05:53.260 --> 00:05:57.080
getting kicked out of Columbia university
for, well,

00:05:57.080 --> 00:05:58.700
being bullshit artists.

00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:02.300
They're forced to start their own risky business
venture,

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:05.560
filling what they believe is an undeserved
market niche.

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:08.260
We're ready to believe you!

00:06:08.260 --> 00:06:11.660
Even though New York is literally being invaded
by evil spirits,

00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:15.780
the true villain of the movie is not the otherworldly
demon Gozer,

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:19.960
but an Environmental Protection Agency bureaucrat
named Walter Peck.

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:21.840
I want this man arrested!

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:25.620
Captain these men are in criminal violation
of the Environmental Protection Act

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:27.500
and this explosion is a direct result of it.

00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:28.500
Your mother!

00:06:28.820 --> 00:06:32.640
But of course the Ghostbusters ultimately
persevere, save the city

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:34.980
and turn a good buck in the process.

00:06:34.980 --> 00:06:38.160
We're gonna have to ask you for four big ones,
4000 dollars for that,

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:41.320
but we are having a special this week on proton
charging...

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:42.520
Well what do you think?

00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:45.020
That was totally wicked!

00:06:45.020 --> 00:06:46.660
It's by no means an exhaustive list,

00:06:46.660 --> 00:06:49.240
so add your personal favorites in the comments.

00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:52.360
And don't forget to tell us just how wrong
we are either.

